**Sec. 70501**
This section terminates the previously-owned clean vehicle credit by amending Section 25E(g) of the Internal Revenue Code. The credit is no longer available for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025, replacing the previous expiration date of December 31, 2032.

**Sec. 70502**
This section terminates the clean vehicle credit for new vehicles by amending Section 30D(h) to disallow credits for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025, instead of the prior December 31, 2032 deadline. It also removes specific eligibility criteria related to battery component sourcing and critical mineral requirements from Section 30D(e).

**Sec. 70503**
This section terminates the qualified commercial clean vehicles credit by amending Section 45W(g). The credit is no longer available for vehicles placed in service after September 30, 2025, replacing the previous expiration date of December 31, 2032.

**Sec. 70504**
This section terminates the alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit by amending Section 30C(i). The credit is no longer available for property placed in service after June 30, 2026, replacing the previous expiration date of December 31, 2032.

**Sec. 70505**
This section terminates the energy efficient home improvement credit by amending Section 25C(h) to disallow credits for property placed in service after December 31, 2025. It also modifies the efficiency criteria for oil furnaces and hot water boilers to require meeting 2021 Energy Star standards and compatibility with fuel blends containing at least 20 percent eligible fuel.

**Sec. 70506**
This section terminates the residential clean energy credit by amending Section 25D(h) to disallow credits for expenditures made after December 31, 2025. It also simplifies the credit rate structure by removing the tiered percentages for years after 2032, effectively ending the credit for future expenditures.

**Sec. 70507**
This section terminates the energy efficient commercial buildings deduction by adding a new subsection to Section 179D. The deduction is no longer available for property where construction begins after June 30, 2026.

**Sec. 70508**
This section terminates the new energy efficient home credit by amending Section 45L(h). The credit is no longer available for homes acquired after June 30, 2026, replacing the previous expiration date of December 31, 2032.

**Sec. 70509**
This section terminates cost recovery for certain energy property by amending Section 168(e)(3)(B)(vi) to remove specific subclauses related to energy property definitions. These amendments apply to property where construction begins after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70510**
This section modifies the zero-emission nuclear power production credit by prohibiting the credit for taxpayers who are specified foreign entities or foreign-influenced entities. The restriction on specified foreign entities applies immediately, while the restriction on foreign-influenced entities applies two years after enactment.

**Sec. 70511**
This section terminates the clean hydrogen production credit by amending Section 45V(c)(3)(C). The credit is no longer available for hydrogen produced after January 1, 2028, replacing the previous expiration date of January 1, 2033.

**Sec. 70512**
This section terminates the clean electricity production credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and imposes strict restrictions on credits for taxpayers or facilities involving prohibited foreign entities. It defines "prohibited foreign entity" to include specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities, establishing material assistance cost ratios that increase annually from 40 percent in 2026 to 60 percent after 2029 for facilities. The section also denies credits for wind and solar leasing arrangements, extends the statute of limitations for errors related to foreign entity determinations to six years, and imposes accuracy-related penalties for substantial understatements due to disallowed credits. Additionally, it creates a new penalty for suppliers who provide false certifications regarding the foreign origin of components, with penalties equal to the greater of 10 percent of the underpayment or $5,000.

**Sec. 70513**
This section terminates the clean electricity investment credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and imposes similar restrictions on prohibited foreign entities as Section 70512. It establishes domestic content bonus percentages that increase from 40 percent for construction beginning before June 16, 2025, to 55 percent for construction beginning after December 31, 2026. The section also denies credits for wind and solar leasing arrangements, eliminates the energy credit for certain energy property by setting the energy percentage to zero, and applies the clean electricity investment credit to qualified fuel cell property at a fixed 30 percent rate.

**Sec. 70514**
This section phases out and restricts the advanced manufacturing production credit by terminating it for wind energy components after December 31, 2027, and for metallurgical coal after December 31, 2029. It phases out credits for other applicable critical minerals starting in 2031, reducing the credit to 75 percent in 2031, 50 percent in 2032, 25 percent in 2033, and zero thereafter. The section imposes restrictions on prohibited foreign entities, modifies the definition of battery modules to include essential equipment like current collector assemblies, and adds metallurgical coal as an eligible critical mineral with a 2.5 percent credit rate.

**Sec. 70515**
This section restricts the extension of the advanced energy project credit program by amending Section 48C(e)(3)(C) to state that the credit amount shall not be increased. This change takes effect on the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70521**
This section extends the clean fuel production credit to December 31, 2029, and prohibits the credit for fuels derived from feedstocks produced outside the United States, Mexico, or Canada. It prohibits negative emission rates for transportation fuels, excludes indirect land use change emissions from calculations, and provides distinct emission rates for fuels derived from animal manure. The section also eliminates the special rate for sustainable aviation fuel, terminates the sustainable aviation fuel credit after September 30, 2025, and extends the small agri-biodiesel producer credit to December 31, 2026, while prohibiting credits for taxpayers who are specified or foreign-influenced entities.

**Sec. 70522**
This section restricts the carbon oxide sequestration credit by prohibiting it for taxpayers who are specified foreign entities or foreign-influenced entities. It establishes parity for different uses of qualified carbon oxide by setting a uniform credit amount of $17 per metric ton for disposal in secure geological storage, with an inflation adjustment factor applied after 2026. The section also adjusts the credit amount for tertiary injectant use to $36 per metric ton.

**Sec. 70523**
This section requires intangible drilling and development costs to be taken into account for purposes of computing adjusted financial statement income for the corporate alternative minimum tax. It amends Section 56A(c)(13) to allow reductions for depreciation and depletion expenses related to these costs, applying to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70524**
This section adds income from hydrogen storage, carbon capture, advanced nuclear, hydropower, and geothermal energy to the qualifying income of certain publicly traded partnerships. It amends Section 7704(d)(1)(E) to include income from the transportation or storage of liquified or compressed hydrogen, carbon capture facilities, advanced nuclear facilities, and specific energy resources. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70525**
This section allows for payments to individuals who remove eligible indelibly dyed diesel fuel or kerosene from a terminal, provided the fuel was previously taxed and is exempt from further taxation. It creates a new Section 6435 in the Internal Revenue Code to facilitate these refunds, applying to fuel removed on or after 180 days after enactment.

**Sec. 70531**
This section modifies the de minimis entry privilege for commercial shipments by imposing civil penalties of up to $5,000 for the first violation and $10,000 for subsequent violations of customs laws. It repeals the commercial shipment exception under Section 321(a)(2) of the Tariff Act of 1930, effective July 1, 2027.

**Sec. 70601**
This section makes the limitation on excess business losses of noncorporate taxpayers permanent by removing the sunset date of January 1, 2029. It also adjusts the calculation amounts for excess business losses by updating the reference dates from December 31, 2018, to December 31, 2025, and from 2017 to 2024.

**Sec. 70602**
This section modifies the treatment of payments from partnerships to partners for property or services by amending Section 707(a)(2). It changes the standard from following prescribed regulations to a general rule, applying to services performed and property transferred after enactment.

**Sec. 70603**
This section applies the $1 million deduction limit for excessive employee remuneration to controlled group members by amending Section 162(m). It requires the limit to be allocated among members of a controlled group based on the proportion of remuneration each member provides to a specified covered employee.

**Sec. 70604**
This section imposes a 1 percent excise tax on certain remittance transfers paid by the sender, collected by the remittance transfer provider. The tax applies only to transfers funded with cash, money orders, cashier's checks, or similar physical instruments, excluding transfers funded by bank accounts or debit/credit cards.

**Sec. 70605**
This section imposes a $1,000 penalty on COVID-ERTC promoters who fail to comply with due diligence requirements and restricts credits and refunds to claims filed by January 31, 2024. It extends the statute of limitations for assessing amounts attributable to improperly claimed credits to six years and expands the penalty for erroneous claims to include employment taxes.

**Sec. 70606**
This section requires taxpayers to include their Social Security number and the Social Security number of any student for whom they claim the American Opportunity or Lifetime Learning credits. It also requires the employer identification number of the educational institution for the American Opportunity Tax Credit, treating omissions as mathematical errors.

**Sec. 70607**
This section appropriates $15 million for the Department of the Treasury to deliver a report to Congress within 90 days on replacing the IRS Direct File program. The report must assess the cost of public-private partnerships for free tax filing, taxpayer preferences, feasibility of new approaches, and the cost of developing a free direct e-file system. [end of text]


